# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Private routes carry X-Robots-Tag: noindex so they can never be indexed even
# when a crawler reaches them despite the robots.txt Disallow — a shared link,
# a fetch, or an agent that renders the SPA. robots.txt alone cannot express
# this: Disallow blocks crawling, but a blocked URL can still be indexed
# (without content) if something links to it, and a crawler that is blocked
# never sees the page's own noindex. A response header reaches every crawler
# and needs no JavaScript.
#
# These paths are served the shared index.html, whose static <meta name="robots">
# says "index, follow" — that default is for the public pages, and it cannot be
# varied per route in a file the SPA reuses. Where the header and the meta
# disagree, Google takes the more restrictive directive, so the noindex here
# wins. The client-side SPA also sets noindex on mount (src/lib/meta.js
# setNoIndex), so the two agree once the JS has run.

/login
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/forgot-password
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/reset*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/portal
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
/portal/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
